WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.340
- If it was possible to

00:00:01.340 --> 00:00:03.842
take out my human heart and hand it over,

00:00:03.842 --> 00:00:06.840
I would give it to the great Billy Porter.

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:07.912
Please say hello.

00:00:07.912 --> 00:00:08.853
(audience applauding)
(screaming)

00:00:08.853 --> 00:00:11.436
(upbeat music)

00:00:15.544 --> 00:00:17.069
- I know you can't touch me

00:00:17.069 --> 00:00:18.128
- I know, I know

00:00:18.128 --> 00:00:19.006
- I hate it.

00:00:19.006 --> 00:00:19.839
- I hate it, too.

00:00:19.839 --> 00:00:21.520
(Laughter)

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:23.790
- Lee Harris and I picked out this outfit.

00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:25.570
- We kinda have on the same shoes

00:00:25.570 --> 00:00:27.250
- I know, by the way.

00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:28.370
- Warrior on the back.

00:00:28.370 --> 00:00:29.370
Ruthie Davis come on.

00:00:29.370 --> 00:00:30.203
- Oh.

00:00:30.203 --> 00:00:31.036
- Come on here.

00:00:31.036 --> 00:00:32.350
- Those are amazing.

00:00:32.350 --> 00:00:34.850
- Your dear friend
Christian Siriano was here.

00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:36.510
- I'm gonna see him tomorrow.

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:39.145
- I think the outfit that
the two of you created

00:00:39.145 --> 00:00:42.900
might be one of the most historic

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:45.465
fashion moments in modern history.

00:00:45.465 --> 00:00:46.298
- [Billy] Thank you.
- [Drew] It's true.

00:00:46.298 --> 00:00:47.131
- [Billy] Thank you.I appreciate that.

00:00:47.131 --> 00:00:48.560
- [Drew] I mean, there has
never been anything like it,

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:51.560
it was the most original
outside the box thinking,

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:54.740
and yet it looked like
it had always been there.

00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:58.150
It was one of those aha
moments if I've ever seen one.

00:00:58.150 --> 00:00:58.983
- [Billy] Well, you know,

00:00:58.983 --> 00:01:00.710
what's interesting about
that for me is that,

00:01:00.710 --> 00:01:03.530
being in drama school with
all my friends and stuff,

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:06.150
you know, we would like talk about it.

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:08.130
Oh, I'm going to wear
a gown to the Oscars.

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:10.660
I don't understand why men only
get to wear that, you know,

00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:13.230
wear suits, the penguin suit,
blah, blah, blah, blah, blah.

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:17.740
And you know, it's rooted
in this masculinity thing,

00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:21.610
you know, masculinity being
the top of the food chain.

00:01:21.610 --> 00:01:25.370
And as a man, as a
black man, particularly,

00:01:25.370 --> 00:01:26.870
if you can't

00:01:26.870 --> 00:01:28.370
inhabit

00:01:28.370 --> 00:01:29.810
masculinity

00:01:29.810 --> 00:01:34.810
in the way that society expects
you to or requires you to,

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:37.200
it's a problem.

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:42.200
And so I find that there are
many men who on the red carpet

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:44.102
would play, you know,

00:01:44.102 --> 00:01:47.360
would have fun if it weren't for

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:50.320
the masculinity gotcha, gotcha.

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:52.990
So I plucked myself out of that game.

00:01:52.990 --> 00:01:55.490
- I know what it's like to be

00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:57.018
pigeonholed as a certain thing.

00:01:57.018 --> 00:01:57.851
- [Billy] Yeah.

00:01:57.851 --> 00:01:59.394
- When I was like a child actor,

00:01:59.394 --> 00:02:00.227
- [ Billy] Yeah.

00:02:00.227 --> 00:02:01.650
- Then I was a bad girl.

00:02:01.650 --> 00:02:04.840
- And you have broken
through every stereotype

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:07.690
or every narrative that
you thought might be a,

00:02:07.690 --> 00:02:12.380
a trapping and created your
own world, let alone a novel.

00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:14.730
- Well, you know, I have to say,

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:17.063
I'm so grateful for the time.

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:22.240
You know, I think we, in
our culture specifically,

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:24.210
everything's a rush, you know.

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:25.079
We're in this microwave life.

00:02:25.079 --> 00:02:26.420
(fingers snapping)
- [Drew] More and more.

00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:27.696
- We're this more quick, faster.

00:02:27.696 --> 00:02:29.480
(mumbles)
- It has to happen.

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:30.313
- Convenience

00:02:30.313 --> 00:02:32.770
- And I took myself out of

00:02:32.770 --> 00:02:35.560
it needed to happen by
such a certain date.

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:38.840
And living in that space was just like

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:40.470
one foot in front of the other,

00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:45.170
leaning into my authenticity
that everybody told me

00:02:45.170 --> 00:02:47.520
was gonna be my liability.

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:50.380
And it was my liability

00:02:50.380 --> 00:02:51.750
for decades.

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:53.570
- You said something.
- Until it wasn't.

00:02:53.570 --> 00:02:57.250
- That really was it
a very important thing

00:02:57.250 --> 00:02:58.700
for myself to hear,

00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:01.410
who is all about self improvement and

00:03:01.410 --> 00:03:03.948
I'm not very kind to myself
as I'm going through it.

00:03:03.948 --> 00:03:07.980
- I got you. I'm learning
self-compassion myself.

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:10.010
I've been working on it during quarantine.

00:03:10.010 --> 00:03:11.550
- And good for you.
(applauding)

00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:14.710
And I'm so good with other people, like.

00:03:14.710 --> 00:03:15.660
- Always with other people.

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:18.270
- I would never treat everyone
on the way I treat myself.

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:19.420
- Which is ridiculous.

00:03:19.420 --> 00:03:20.890
- Which is nuts.

00:03:20.890 --> 00:03:21.723
- Yeah.

00:03:21.723 --> 00:03:25.760
- But you said there is a
quote that you chose you.

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:29.640
What was choosing you? What
did that ultimately feel like?

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:31.940
Because to me it's not as
self-congratulatory thing,

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:33.323
it's a very brave thing.

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:35.910
- Well, you know,

00:03:35.910 --> 00:03:39.340
I was in the valley and I was
in the valley for a long time.

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:41.560
You know, I always say
it's easy to be who you are

00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:43.360
when what you are is what's popular.

00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:48.270
Who I, who I am was not popular.

00:03:48.270 --> 00:03:50.330
And I just said, you know what?

00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:52.990
This is what it is. This is my truth.

00:03:52.990 --> 00:03:55.950
And whatever comes of this is

00:03:55.950 --> 00:03:58.370
what's supposed to come of this.

00:03:58.370 --> 00:04:02.080
- Today is very special
for us. And, you know,

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:03.770
I was reading about how, when you were

00:04:03.770 --> 00:04:06.140
in like the Vogue scene and you know,

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:10.770
I was studio 54, we sort
of had this bon vivant

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:14.592
like wonderful cultural upbringing of

00:04:14.592 --> 00:04:17.290
living and expression

00:04:17.290 --> 00:04:18.930
and people, you know.

00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:22.040
Their hearts are sort of
like these shooting stars

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:25.250
very much like the set
you're about to step on

00:04:25.250 --> 00:04:29.040
for our first inaugural Sing to a Flower.

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:30.400
- I love Sing to a Flower

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:34.474
- Well, this idea is gifted
to us by none other than

00:04:34.474 --> 00:04:36.400
Jimmy Fallon,

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:38.730
who he's married to my business partner,

00:04:38.730 --> 00:04:41.410
Nancy Juvonen Fallon of 25 years,

00:04:41.410 --> 00:04:42.940
we built Flower Films together.

00:04:42.940 --> 00:04:44.230
So we've made everything together.

00:04:44.230 --> 00:04:46.910
And he said, I have to
gift you guys this idea,

00:04:46.910 --> 00:04:49.290
because it turns out that
if you sing to a flower,

00:04:49.290 --> 00:04:53.450
there is scientific evidence
that it helps it grow.

00:04:53.450 --> 00:04:57.250
And it was only a seed of
an idea, pun intended then,

00:04:57.250 --> 00:04:58.083
(light laughter)

00:04:58.083 --> 00:05:02.500
but here we are with Billy
Porter about to sing a song,

00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:05.870
which I would love for you
to tell us about, edelweiss.

00:05:05.870 --> 00:05:07.770
- You know, it's a flower that blooms

00:05:07.770 --> 00:05:11.360
in the harshest of environments. You know,

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:16.140
and it teaches us that
we can and must flourish

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:17.300
in the darkest of times.

00:05:17.300 --> 00:05:21.360
And I know that this is a really
dark time for a lot of us.

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:25.210
And you know, this song
is a, is a song of hope.

00:05:25.210 --> 00:05:28.160
It's a song of peace and of love.

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:30.430
That's all we got right now.

00:05:30.430 --> 00:05:32.170
You know, that's all we can do is

00:05:32.170 --> 00:05:37.013
show up and make sure that
we represent the change

00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:42.490
as individuals that we want
to see as the collective.

00:05:42.490 --> 00:05:45.090
- Well, between Jimmy Fallon's gift to us

00:05:45.090 --> 00:05:46.330
and his seed of an idea

00:05:46.330 --> 00:05:49.300
and what you met it with
with picking this song

00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:52.430
that lyrically is so apropos of this year

00:05:52.430 --> 00:05:54.100
and of this moment,

00:05:54.100 --> 00:05:56.750
I think your two ideas come
together and it's like,

00:05:56.750 --> 00:05:58.862
candide Voltaire, cultivate the garden.

00:05:58.862 --> 00:06:00.300
(laughter)

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:02.850
Let's, let's kick this off in
the most extraordinary way.

00:06:02.850 --> 00:06:05.792
That's so beyond our wildest dreams,

00:06:05.792 --> 00:06:08.375
(upbeat music)

